WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.940
If you’ve walked or driven around Washington,
DC, you’ve probably noticed these things.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.060
Cast-iron boxes installed on street corners
all over the city, sometimes with art or a

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.840
little neighborhood history inside.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.320
They’re clearly pretty old and not being
used for their original purpose anymore.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.519
But what are they?

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.400
And why are they everywhere?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.140
It turns out these cast-iron fixtures were
vital for rapid communication in cities like

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.660
Washington before the telephone was invented.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.300
and they're really old.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.460
Some of the original fire boxes, like this
one in DC’s Cleveland Park, even date back

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:39.860
to the 1860s.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.230
You can tell the original ones by their harp
shape.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:47.640
By 1890, the Gamewell Fire Alarm Telegraph
Co. had installed call box systems in 500

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.210
American cities.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:53.010
This map from 1891 shows where they were installed
throughout DC.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.070
The red dots are fire boxes, and the blue
dots are police boxes.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.910
If you saw a fire in your own house or on
the block, you could run down to the corner

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.560
and pull the fire alarm key.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.940
A huge network of underground cables connected
every box in the city to the central alarm

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.840
center.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.400
There is a fire alarm box on every block of
the great city.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.270
The number that the box would tap out matched
a location on a giant map, so the fire department

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:20.250
was able to pretty quickly figure out where
to send fire engines.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.910
It could be nothing serious.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.600
It could be a false alarm.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.580
The police boxes worked a bit differently
than the fire boxes.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.080
They came a little later and had an oval shape.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.270
Like the fire boxes, the police boxes had
numbers corresponding to their location.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.701
And police stations expected to hear from
their officers on patrol from a different

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:50.600
box at scheduled times.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.300
Officers would check in and say all is well,
or they could use the box to call for backup

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:57.060
or receive updated orders for their patrol
area.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.720
Using the call box system, the Metropolitan
Police Department was able to keep track of

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.810
their officers throughout the city before
the invention of two-way radio.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.030
Technology eventually caught up, though.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.580
More people got phones in their homes, and
the 911 system was invented in the 1970s to

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.590
link the fire and police departments.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.740
The boxes slowly lost their purpose.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.000
But they’re not totally obsolete just yet.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.360
In cities like San Francisco and Boston, for
example, you can still reach the fire department

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:23.360
through telegraph.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.420
In situations like earthquakes and floods
when power and cellphone services are knocked

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.860
out, these boxes are still reliably connected
to emergency services.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.380
Here in DC, the call boxes were abandoned
in the late ’70s.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.540
And they were just kind of … left in place
because it was cheaper and easier just to

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.940
leave them in place.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.340
And then people just kind of forgot about
them.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.060
They took off the boxes themselves that had
the little telephone in it.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.960
So it just kind of looked like a little blank
piece of Victoriana street furniture that

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.400
... a lot of people had no idea what that
was.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:02.070
But that changed in 2000 when members of the
nonprofit Cultural Tourism DC founded a project

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.620
called “Art on Call.”

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.140
They gave city officials a cheaper and more
creative way to deal with the iron relics:

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.400
turn them into art.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.220
The city liked the idea, and even helped out
by stripping the lead paint and priming over

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.160
700 boxes before handing them over to the
neighborhoods.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.320
The idea was not to just refurbish the boxes,
but for neighborhoods to come together and

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.240
decide on what they wanted their call boxes
to look like.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.780
These call boxes have watched over the same
street corners for more than 100 years.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.580
And even though they’re not saving lives
anymore, they continue to serve their communities

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.940
as carriers of culture and local history.

